# Single-page app fallback.
#
# The portal addresses itself with REAL paths via history.pushState (/brief,
# /story/<id>, /trend/<id>, /signal/<id>, /trends, /saved, /read, /profile) —
# deliberately, so search engines and shared links see one canonical URL per
# story instead of everything collapsing to "#".
#
# The cost is that the host must hand back index.html for any path it has no
# file for. Client-side routing only starts AFTER index.html loads, so on a
# refresh or a pasted link the browser asks the host for /brief directly; with
# no rule here that is a 404 from the host ("Cannot GET /brief") and the app
# never boots. It bites logged-out visitors hardest because they are the ones
# arriving on shared links rather than already having a tab open.
#
# Status 200 (not 301/302): rewrite, don't redirect, so the address bar keeps
# the real URL for the router to read. Real files — index.html, icon.svg,
# og.png, robots.txt — are served normally and still take precedence.
#
# Read by Netlify, Cloudflare Pages and Render static sites. Other hosts need
# the same rule expressed their own way:
#   Vercel   vercel.json -> {"rewrites":[{"source":"/(.*)","destination":"/index.html"}]}
#   Express  app.get('*', (req,res) => res.sendFile(__dirname + '/index.html'))
#   nginx    location / { try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html; }
#   serve    serve -s web        (the -s flag IS this rule)
/*    /index.html   200
